{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And he fashioned it with a graving-tool.</b> It can be translated in two ways. One: וַיָּצַר — a term of binding; בַּחֶרֶט — a term for a cloth, as in ״וְהַמִּטְפָּחוֹת וְהָחֲרִיטִים״ (Isaiah 3), ״וַיָּצַר כִּכְּרַיִם כֶּסֶף בִּשְׁנֵי חֲרִיטִים״ (II Kings 5). The second: וַיָּצַר — a term of form; בַּחֶרֶט — a tool of the goldsmiths' craft, with which they engrave and incise forms in gold, like a scribe's pen that incises letters on tablets and in ledgers, as in ״וּכְתֹב עָלָיו בְּחֶרֶט אֱנוֹשׁ״ (Isaiah 8). And this is what Onkelos translated: וְצַר יָתֵיהּ בְּזִיפָא — a term of engraving (זִיּוּף); it is a tool of craft with which they engrave in gold letters and ornaments (שְׁקֵדִים), which are called in la'az ניי\"ל, and they engrave with it seals." "<b>A molten calf.</b> Once he cast it into the fire of the crucible, sorcerers of the mixed multitude that came up with them from Egypt arrived and made it by sorcery. And some say Mikhah was there — he emerged from the rubble of the building that was crushed in it in Egypt, and in his hand was a plate on which Moses had written 'עֲלֵה שׁוֹר, עֲלֵה שׁוֹר' ('Go up, ox') to bring up the coffin of Joseph from the Nile — and he cast it into the crucible and the calf came out (Tanhuma)." "<b>Molten.</b> A term for molten metal (מַתֶּכֶת). Another interpretation: 125 kantarin of gold were in it, like the numerology of מַסֵּכָה (there)." "<b>These are your elohim.</b> And it was not said 'These are our elohim' — from here: the mixed multitude that came up from Egypt are the ones who gathered against Aaron and they are the ones who made it, and afterward led Israel astray after it (there)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "Onkelos reads בְּזִיפָא; Rashi equates this Aramaic with Hebrew זִיּוּף, a graving-tool. שְׁקֵדִים are almond-shaped ornaments (cf. Exodus 25:33-34). ניי\"ל is Old French.", 1 "Mikhah, identified in Tanhuma with the idol of Mikhah in Judges 17-18.", 2 "קכ\"ה = 125 in gematria; קַנְטְרִין are hundreds (centenaria), a unit of weight."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:56:14.477171427Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}